				LIST-VIEWERS

A "list-viewer" is a class that can be the basis for a wide variety of
scrolling-list style user interface components.  A list-viewer is like a
scrolling-list in that it displays a potentially large number of
choices, stacked vertically, in a window with a vertical scrollbar.
List-Viewers are different than scrolling-lists:

- Unlike scrolling-lists each choice is drawn by the application.  As a result
choices are not constrained to be strings or a small glyph followed by a string;
list-viewer choices can be anything your application can draw on a canvas or an image.

- The height and width of list-viewer choices can vary, scrolling-list choices must
all have the same width and height.

- The cost of creating a list-viewer is proportional to the number of viewable
choices rather than the total number of choices.

- Although both scrolling-lists and list-viewers are designed to display lists
of choices where each choice occupies one line or "row", list-viewers can be
extended to support columns or or some other tessellation of the space within 
a row.


The LispView list-viewer package defines and exports three basic
list-viewer classes: list-viewer, exclusive-list-viewer and
non-exclusive-list-viewer.  The second two classes are subclasses of
list-viewer; they provide basic definitions for the methods
select-choice and highlight-choice (see below).  To create a new kind
of list-viewer you need to define a subclass of one of the exported
classes and methods for (at least): lv-nth-choice, lv-draw-choice, lv-choice-height.


LV-N-CHOICES (v list-viewer)

Returns the total, fixnum, number of choices to be displayed in the list-viewer.
You can change this value at any time with setf.  


LV-NTH-CHOICE (v list-viewer) row

Returns the object that represents the choice for the specified row and list-viewer.
The list-viewer implementation will apply the remaining four methods to this object
to determine its dimensions, draw it, highlight it, and handle mouse events that
occur within it.  There is no default method for lv-nth-choice.


LV-CHOICE-HEIGHT (v list-viewer) choice

Return a distance large enough to contain the entire choice when
rendered by lv-draw-choice and possibly highlighted by lv-highlight-choice.
There is no default method for lv-choice-height.


LV-DRAW-CHOICE (v list-viewer) drawable x y choice row

Draw the choice on the specified drawable so that the upper left hand
corner of the choices outermost region is at x,y.  When the choice is
drawn it should be no higher than the value returned by lv-choice-height.
There is no default method for lv-draw-choice.


LV-HIGHLIGHT-CHOICE (v list-viewer) drawable region choice row operation

This method will be called after a choice has been drawn so that
the application can add a mark to indicate that the choice has been selected.
Operation will be :draw or :erase, region will be the portion of drawable
that contains the choice and any existing highlight.   The default method 
for highlight-choice draws or erases a rectangle on the edges of region.


LV-SELECT-CHOICE (v list-viewer) canvas x y choice row

This method is called whenever the select button (the left button) is 
pressed within the list-viewer.  The coordinates of mouse, x,y, are passed
along for applications that want to subdivide a single row into more than
one mouse sensitive area.  There is no default method for select-choice
however there are predefined methods for select-choice specialized for
exclusive-list-viewer and non-exclusive-list-viewer.



The rest of the list-viewer interface is equally flexible however most
applications will probably be able to use the standard definitions.
Note that although list-viewer is NOT a subclass of canvas it does
support some of the basic canvas protocol: display, status,
bounding-region, mapped, parent interests.  The semantics of the
list-viewer methods for these generic functions are the same as for
canvases and are not documented here.



INITIALIZE-INSTANCE (v list-viewer) 
	            &key 
	              parent mapped interests
	              bounding-region left right top bottom width height
		      view-width view-height view-region first-viewable-row 
		      foreground background
                    &allow-other-keys

Create a new list-viewer.  The list-viewer will include a complex of
one or more windows, usually a display window and a scrollbar, all of
the windows will share the specified parent.  The bounding-region
arguments specify the outer dimensions of the entire list-viewer
window complex.  View-width and view-height or view-region specify the inner size of
the list-viewers display window.  Foreground and background apply to the 
list-viewers display window.


LV-VIEWABLE-CHOICES (v list-viewer)
LV-CHOICE-OBJECT x
LV-CHOICE-ROW x
LV-CHOICE-HIGHLIGHT x
LV-CHOICE-REGION x

Lv-viewable-choices returns a list of the objects that represents
the choices that are at least partially visible in the list-viewers
display window.  The remaining functions can be applied to these objects:
lv-choice-object returns the actual choice object, lv-choice-row returns
the choices row number, lv-choice-highlight returns non NIL if the choice
has been highlighted, lv-choice-region returns the region in the list-viewers
viewport where the choice is currently displayed.  None of these values
can be changed with setf.



LV-FIRST-VIEWABLE-ROW (v list-viewer)

Return the row number of the first at least partially viewable row or change it
with setf.  If there are now rows, e.g. if lv-n-choices = 0, then return 0.



LV-UPDATE-CHOICES (v list-viewer) &key n-choices first-viewable-row

Force the viewer to recompute and redraw it's entire display.  This is useful
if you've created a viewer subclass whose entire set of choices may change
periodically.   The default for n-choices is the value of (lv-nchoices v)
and the default for first-viewable-row is the smaller of (lv-nchoices v) 
and (1- nchoices).



LV-SELECT-CHOICE (v exclusive-list-viewer) canvas x y choice row
LV-SELECT-CHOICE (v non-exclusive-list-viewer) canvas x y choice row

Both methods change the list-viewers value with setf.



VIEWPORT (v list-viewer) 

Return the viewers display window.  The value of viewport can not be changed with
setf.  Note that the viewport isn't necessarily the drawable passed to lv-draw-choice
and lv-highlight-choice.  To find the the list-viewer associated with a display-window
apply the method list-viewer to the window.  In other words (list-viewer (viewport x)) => x.
This is only important for applications that define their own interests and
receive-event methods for a viewer.


VIEW-REGION (v list-viewer)

Return the inner dimensions of the list-viewers display or change them with setf.
The setf method for view-region only uses the regions width and height.


VALUE (v exclusive-list-viewer)
VALUE (v non-exclusive-list-viewer)

The exclusive-list-viewer value method returns the most recently selected
choice.  The non-exclusive-list-viewer method returns a list of all of the selected 
choices.  In both cases changing the value with setf causes highlight-choice
to be called to :draw and :erase highlight marks for the affected choices.


MENU

The menu to be popped up when the user presses the menu button (the right
button) over the list-viewer.  [not supported yet]
